Based on deep investigation of the Microsoft Agent Framework source code, here are the two primary patterns for handling this in .NET, plus the memory-cleanup mechanisms.

---

## 1. Delegating Multiple Independent Lookups in Parallel

The framework provides **two distinct approaches**, each suited to a different level of control:

### Approach A: `SubAgentsProvider` — Agent-Level Delegation

The parent agent is given a list of sub-agents via `SubAgentsProvider`, which exposes tools the LLM calls directly. Each sub-task runs in its own `AgentSession` via `Task.Run`, so they execute concurrently:

```csharp
// dotnet/samples/02-agents/Harness/Harness_Step02_Research_WithSubAgents/Program.cs
var parentAgent = new OpenAIClient(...)
    .GetResponsesClient()
    .AsIChatClientWithStoredOutputDisabled(deploymentName)
    .AsHarnessAgent(MaxContextWindowTokens, MaxOutputTokens, new HarnessAgentOptions
    {
        Name = "StockPriceResearcher",
        AIContextProviders = [ new SubAgentsProvider([webSearchAgent]) ],
    });
```

The `SubAgentsProvider` (at `dotnet/src/Microsoft.Agents.AI/Harness/SubAgents/SubAgentsProvider.cs`) exposes six tools to the parent agent's LLM:

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `SubAgents_StartTask` | Start a sub-task on a named agent; returns task ID |
| `SubAgents_WaitForFirstCompletion` | Block until the first of specified tasks completes |
| `SubAgents_GetTaskResults` | Retrieve text output of a completed sub-task |
| `SubAgents_GetAllTasks` | List all tasks with IDs, statuses, agent names |
| `SubAgents_ContinueTask` | Send follow-up input to a completed/failed task's session |
| `SubAgents_ClearCompletedTask` | Remove a completed task and release its session |

Internally, each `StartTask` call does:

```csharp
// SubAgentsProvider.cs, lines 244-252
AgentSession subSession = await agent.CreateSessionAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
runtimeState.InFlightTasks[taskId] = Task.Run(() => agent.RunAsync(input, subSession));
runtimeState.SubTaskSessions[taskId] = subSession;
```

The parent agent's instructions guide the LLM to start **all** sub-tasks before waiting:

```csharp
// Harness_Step02_Research_WithSubAgents/Program.cs, lines 56-76
"You are a stock price research assistant...
1. For each ticker, start a sub-task on the WebSearchAgent...
   - Start all sub-tasks before waiting for any of them to complete, so they run concurrently.
2. Wait for all sub-tasks to complete.
3. Retrieve the results from each sub-task.
4. Present a summary table...
5. Clear all completed tasks to free memory."
```

### Approach B: Workflows — Structured Fan-Out/Fan-In

For more deterministic orchestration, use the workflow engine with `ConcurrentStartExecutor`, `AddFanOutEdge`, and `AddFanInBarrierEdge`:

```csharp
// dotnet/samples/03-workflows/Concurrent/Concurrent/Program.cs, lines 55-63
var startExecutor = new ConcurrentStartExecutor();
var aggregationExecutor = new ConcurrentAggregationExecutor();

var workflow = new WorkflowBuilder(startExecutor)
    .AddFanOutEdge(startExecutor, [physicist, chemist])
    .AddFanInBarrierEdge([physicist, chemist], aggregationExecutor)
    .WithOutputFrom(aggregationExecutor)
    .Build();

await using StreamingRun run = await InProcessExecution.RunStreamingAsync(workflow, input: "What is temperature?");
```

The `ConcurrentEndExecutor` (at `dotnet/src/Microsoft.Agents.AI.Workflows/Specialized/ConcurrentEndExecutor.cs`) acts as a barrier — it counts incoming results and only yields output when all expected inputs arrive:

```csharp
// ConcurrentEndExecutor.cs, lines 41-59
lock (this._allResults)
{
    this._allResults.Add(messages);
    done = --this._remaining == 0;
}
if (done)
{
    this._remaining = this._expectedInputs;
    var results = this._allResults;
    this._allResults = new List<List<ChatMessage>>(this._expectedInputs);
    await context.YieldOutputAsync(this._aggregator(results), cancellationToken);
}
```

---

## 2. Waiting for Results

### SubAgentsProvider: Iterative `WhenAny`

The `SubAgents_WaitForFirstCompletion` tool uses `Task.WhenAny` to wait for the first task, then the parent agent loops:

```csharp
// SubAgentsProvider.cs, lines 300-313
Task completedTask = await Task.WhenAny(waitableTasks.Select(t => t.Task)).ConfigureAwait(false);
var completedEntry = waitableTasks.First(t => t.Task == completedTask);
SubTaskInfo? taskInfo = state.Tasks.FirstOrDefault(t => t.Id == completedEntry.Id);
if (taskInfo is not null)
{
    FinalizeTask(taskInfo, completedEntry.Task, runtimeState);
    this._sessionState.SaveState(session, state);
}
return $"Task {completedEntry.Id} finished with status: {taskInfo?.Status}.";
```

The parent agent calls `GetTaskResults` for each completed task, then `WaitForFirstCompletion` again for the remaining ones.

### Workflows: Barrier Edge

The `AddFanInBarrierEdge` is a structural barrier — the aggregation executor simply does not receive input until all fan-out agents have completed. No explicit wait code is needed.

---

## 3. Handling Failed or Unclear Work

### SubAgentsProvider: Task Status + Continue

Each sub-task tracks one of four statuses (`SubTaskStatus` enum):

```csharp
// SubAgentsProvider.cs, lines 193-214
private static void FinalizeTask(SubTaskInfo taskInfo, Task<AgentResponse> completedTask, SubAgentRuntimeState runtimeState)
{
    if (completedTask.Status == TaskStatus.RanToCompletion)
    {
        taskInfo.Status = SubTaskStatus.Completed;
        taskInfo.ResultText = completedTask.Result.Text;
    }
    else if (completedTask.IsFaulted)
    {
        taskInfo.Status = SubTaskStatus.Failed;
        taskInfo.ErrorText = completedTask.Exception?.InnerException?.Message ?? "Unknown error";
    }
    else if (completedTask.IsCanceled)
    {
        taskInfo.Status = SubTaskStatus.Failed;
        taskInfo.ErrorText = "Task was canceled.";
    }
}
```

The `SubAgents_ContinueTask` tool lets the parent agent retry a failed or unclear task on the **same session** (preserving conversational context):

```csharp
// SubAgentsProvider.cs, lines 376-415
// Reset task state and start a new run on the existing session.
taskInfo.Status = SubTaskStatus.Running;
taskInfo.ResultText = null;
taskInfo.ErrorText = null;
runtimeState.InFlightTasks[taskId] = Task.Run(() => agent.RunAsync(text, subSession));
```

The parent agent's instructions explicitly handle this:

```csharp
// Harness_Step02_Research_WithSubAgents/Program.cs, line 74
"- If a sub-task fails or returns unclear results, continue the task with a more specific query."
```

### Workflows: `ExecutorFailedEvent`

When an executor throws, the framework emits an `ExecutorFailedEvent`:

```csharp
// dotnet/src/Microsoft.Agents.AI.Workflows/ExecutorFailedEvent.cs
public sealed class ExecutorFailedEvent(string executorId, Exception? err)
    : ExecutorEvent(executorId, data: err)
{
    public new Exception? Data => err;
}
```

Consumers listen via the event stream:

```csharp
// Concurrent/Program.cs, lines 75-83
case ExecutorFailedEvent executorFailed:
    WriteError($"Executor '{executorFailed.ExecutorId}' failed with {executorFailed.Data}.");
    break;
```

The fan-in barrier does **not** automatically continue on failure — a failed executor means the barrier never fires. You would need to implement a custom executor that catches exceptions and yields partial results, or use the SubAgentsProvider pattern where the parent agent has explicit retry logic.

---

## 4. Cleaning Up Memory

### SubAgentsProvider: Explicit `ClearCompletedTask`

The framework provides a dedicated tool for memory cleanup. The parent agent's instructions mandate it:

```csharp
// SubAgentsProvider.cs, lines 424-448
// Remove the task from state.
state.Tasks.Remove(taskInfo);
// Clean up runtime references.
runtimeState.InFlightTasks.Remove(taskId);
runtimeState.SubTaskSessions.Remove(taskId);
```

The default instructions embedded in the provider (line 49) explicitly tell the agent:

> "After retrieving results from a completed task, clear it with SubAgents_ClearCompletedTask to free memory, unless you plan to continue it with SubAgents_ContinueTask."

### Workflows: `IAsyncDisposable` on Run Handle

The `AsyncRunHandle` (at `dotnet/src/Microsoft.Agents.AI.Workflows/Execution/AsyncRunHandle.cs`) implements `IAsyncDisposable`:

```csharp
// AsyncRunHandle.cs, lines 172-185
public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
{
    if (Interlocked.Exchange(ref this._isDisposed, 1) == 0)
    {
        await this.CancelRunAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
        await this._stepRunner.RequestEndRunAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
        this._endRunSource.Dispose();
        await this._eventStream.DisposeAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
    }
}
```

Sample code uses `await using` to ensure cleanup:

```csharp
// Concurrent/Program.cs, line 66
await using StreamingRun run = await InProcessExecution.RunStreamingAsync(workflow, input: "What is temperature?");
```

### Agent-Level Disposal

Agents that wrap clients (e.g., `OpenTelemetryAgent`, `PurviewAgent`, `GitHubCopilotAgent`) implement `IDisposable` or `IAsyncDisposable` to release underlying HTTP clients and telemetry resources. Use `await using` or `using` blocks for agents created in your application:

```csharp
// dotnet/tests/AgentConformance.IntegrationTests/Support/AgentCleanup.cs
internal sealed class AgentCleanup(ChatClientAgent agent, IChatClientAgentFixture fixture) : IAsyncDisposable
{
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync() =>
        await fixture.DeleteAgentAsync(agent);
}
```

---

## Summary: Which Pattern to Choose

| Concern | SubAgentsProvider | Workflows |
|---------|-------------------|-----------|
| **Parallel delegation** | LLM calls `SubAgents_StartTask` for each lookup | `AddFanOutEdge` fans out to all agents |
| **Waiting** | `SubAgents_WaitForFirstCompletion` in a loop | `AddFanInBarrierEdge` blocks until all complete |
| **Failed/unclear work** | `SubAgents_ContinueTask` retries on same session | `ExecutorFailedEvent` — no automatic retry |
| **Memory cleanup** | `SubAgents_ClearCompletedTask` (agent-guided) | `await using StreamingRun` disposes the run handle |
| **Best for** | Flexible, LLM-driven orchestration with retry logic | Deterministic, structured fan-out/fan-in pipelines |

